# Every text file gets an explicit eol. Nothing is left to `* text=auto`.
#
# The fallback is not harmless. `* text=auto` combined with core.autocrlf=true writes CRLF
# into the working tree on a Windows clone, and the template-integrity manifest hashes are
# byte-exact -- that is how Slice 1B shipped a repository whose own tests failed on a fresh
# checkout while every established working tree passed. The same gap has since been found
# three more times, on .tmpl, on .yml, and on .css.
#
# So: adding a new text file type means adding it here in the same commit. An unpinned
# extension is a latent version of that bug, not a tidiness issue.

* text=auto

# Source
*.mjs   text eol=lf
*.ts    text eol=lf
*.tsx   text eol=lf
*.py    text eol=lf
*.css   text eol=lf
*.svg   text eol=lf

# Data, config and documentation
*.json  text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.txt   text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf
.gitattributes text eol=lf
.gitignore     text eol=lf

# Reviewed insertion-page templates. Byte-exact: their SHA-256 is the integrity check.
*.tmpl  text eol=lf

# PowerShell is run by Windows tooling that expects CRLF.
*.ps1   text eol=crlf

# Binary
*.png   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.wav   binary
*.flac  binary
